---
import Doc from "../../layouts/Doc.astro";
import { u, RELEASES } from "../../lib/site";
import { latestRelease, ROWS, cell, sums } from "../../lib/release";

const release = await latestRelease();
---

<Doc
  title="Download Sub20 for Windows and Linux"
  description="Both downloads of Sub20 for each system, the checksums, and what Windows says the first time you open it."
  current="download"
  heading="Download Sub20"
  lead={release
    ? `Version ${release.version}, free, for Windows and Linux. Pick your system, then one of the two downloads.`
    : "Free, for Windows and Linux. Pick your system, then one of the two downloads."}
>
  <h2>Two downloads per system</h2>
  <p><b>Sub20</b> has FFmpeg, which does the compressing, built in. Most people want this one.</p>
  <p><b>Sub20 without FFmpeg</b> is a much smaller download that uses the FFmpeg already on your computer. If there is none, its first screen offers to download the same FFmpeg the other download carries, checked against a pinned SHA-256.</p>
  <p>Both update themselves, each to its own kind, and every update is signed.</p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Your system</th><th scope="col">Sub20</th><th scope="col">Sub20 without FFmpeg</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {ROWS.map((row) => {
          const full = cell(release, row, false);
          const lean = cell(release, row, true);
          return (
            <tr data-os={row.os}>
              <th scope="row">{row.system}<br /><span class="muted">{row.detail}, {row.kind}</span></th>
              <td><a href={full.href}>{full.size ? `Download, ${full.size}` : "Releases page"}</a></td>
              <td><a href={lean.href}>{lean.size ? `Download, ${lean.size}` : "Releases page"}</a></td>
            </tr>
          );
        })}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  {!release && <p class="muted">There is no release yet. Every version will be on the <a href={RELEASES}>releases page</a>.</p>}
  <p>There is no Mac version. On a Mac, <a href={u("app/")}>Sub20 Lite</a> runs in the browser.</p>

  <h2>The first time you open it</h2>
  <p>The Windows build isn't code-signed yet, so Windows warns the first time you open it. Signing is being set up: see the <a href={u("code-signing/")}>code signing policy</a>. Updates are signed either way.</p>
  <h3>Windows: "Windows protected your PC"</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the installer.</li>
    <li>If SmartScreen says "Windows protected your PC", click <b>More info</b>.</li>
    <li>Click <b>Run anyway</b>, then install as usual.</li>
  </ol>
  <h3>Linux: no warning</h3>
  <p>The deb and rpm install like any other package and add Sub20 to your menu. Make the AppImage executable first:</p>
  <p><code>chmod +x Sub20*.AppImage</code></p>

  <h2>Checksums</h2>
  <p>Each release lists the SHA-256 of every file in <a href={sums(release)}>SHA256SUMS.txt</a>. To check a download on Windows, run <code>certutil -hashfile</code> with the file's name and <code>SHA256</code>; on Linux, <code>sha256sum</code> with the file's name. The result should match the file's line.</p>

  <h2>What it needs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><b>Windows</b> 10 or 11, 64-bit.</li>
    <li><b>Linux</b>: 64-bit. The deb and rpm use the system's WebKitGTK; the AppImage carries its own.</li>
    <li>A graphics card is used when there is one (NVIDIA, AMD or Intel). Without one, the processor encodes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Can't install anything? <a href={u("app/")}>Sub20 Lite</a> runs in your browser.</p>
</Doc>
